<section xml:id="sec-mod8-bridge-reading">
  <title>Module 8: Bridge Reading Guide</title>
  <p>Bauldry §1.4 and §2.4 — Estimated time: 40 minutes</p>
    <p>
      Every module ends with a bridge reading in Bauldry's
      <em>Introduction to Real Analysis</em>. For this module the
      reading covers two sections: Bauldry &#xa7;1.4
      (<q>The Integral</q>) and Bauldry &#xa7;2.4
      (<q>The Riemann-Stieltjes Integral</q>). Section &#xa7;1.4
      is Bauldry's informal review of integration, parallel to what
      you have just built via Darboux sums. Section &#xa7;2.4 is the
      rigorous graduate treatment &#x2014; and it goes substantially
      further than Zorn by introducing the Riemann-Stieltjes integral
      <m>\int f\,d\alpha</m>, which replaces the length element
      <m>dx</m> with a more general increment <m>d\alpha</m>,
      determined by an integrator function <m>\alpha</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This generalization is the correct setup for many applications
      in graduate analysis and probability. MAT 5610 will spend
      significant time on Bauldry &#xa7;2.4. The module you have just
      completed gives you the Darboux framework; the graduate course
      adds the Stieltjes integrator. The exercises below help you build
      a bridge between these two presentations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>For this module, read:</alert>
      Bauldry &#xa7;1.4 (<q>The Integral</q>, approximately pp. 18&#x2013;24)
      and &#xa7;2.4 (<q>The Riemann-Stieltjes Integral</q>,
      approximately pp. 71&#x2013;88).
    </p>
  <!-- ============================================================
       PART 1: BAULDRY §1.4
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod8-br-14">
    <title>Bauldry &#xa7;1.4 &#x2014; The Riemann Integral (informal)</title>
    <p>
      Section &#xa7;1.4 is Bauldry's informal treatment of the integral,
      parallel to Zorn &#xa7;5.1&#x2013;5.2. Bauldry introduces the
      integral with an eye toward the rigorous Riemann-Stieltjes theory
      that follows in &#xa7;2.4. Notice whether his setup emphasizes
      Darboux sums, Riemann sums, or both; the choice of approach
      shapes what the FTC proof looks like.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-br-1">
    <title>Bauldry's Definition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What definition of the integral does Bauldry use in &#xa7;1.4
        &#x2014; Riemann sums (with sample points), Darboux sums (with
        sup and inf), or something else? Quote the key definition.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-br-2">
    <title>FTC in Bauldry &#xa7;1.4</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Does Bauldry prove the FTC in &#xa7;1.4, or does he
            merely state it? What level of rigor does he use?
          </li>
          <li>
            Compare the presentation of FTC in &#xa7;1.4 to Worked
            Examples 3 and 4 in this module. What is made explicit in
            the module that is implicit in Bauldry's &#xa7;1.4?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-br-3">
    <title>Beyond Area</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Bauldry &#xa7;1.4 mentions applications of the integral that
        go beyond area computation. Identify one such application and
        describe briefly how the integral is used.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       PART 2: BAULDRY §2.4
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod8-br-24">
    <title>Bauldry &#xa7;2.4 &#x2014; The Riemann-Stieltjes Integral (rigorous)</title>
    <p>
      Section &#xa7;2.4 is the starting point of MAT 5610's integration
      theory. The Riemann-Stieltjes integral <m>\int_a^b f\,d\alpha</m>
      replaces <m>dx</m> (the length element) with <m>d\alpha</m>
      (determined by an <em>integrator</em> function <m>\alpha</m>).
      When <m>\alpha(x) = x</m>, the Riemann-Stieltjes integral reduces
      to the ordinary Riemann integral. The generalization is powerful
      because it unifies the integral with Stieltjes-type weighted
      averages and connects naturally to measure theory.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read &#xa7;2.4 carefully. When the integrator <m>\alpha</m> is
      increasing, the definitions mirror what you have learned (upper
      sums, lower sums, integrability criterion), but with
      <m>\Delta\alpha_i = \alpha(x_i) - \alpha(x_{i-1})</m>
      replacing <m>\Delta x_i</m>. The hypothesis that <m>\alpha</m>
      is increasing matters: it makes every <m>\Delta\alpha_i \geq 0</m>,
      which is what makes upper and lower sums with these weights
      behave like Darboux sums. For an integrator of bounded variation,
      the integral is defined instead through Riemann-Stieltjes sums
      <m>\sum f(t_i)\,\Delta\alpha_i</m> with sample points
      <m>t_i \in [x_{i-1}, x_i]</m>, or reduced to the increasing case by
      writing <m>\alpha</m> as the difference of two increasing
      functions, which is possible for every function of bounded
      variation. (A second source for both topics is Apostol,
      <pubtitle>Mathematical Analysis</pubtitle>, 2nd edition,
      Chapters 6 and 7.) Pay attention to where the hypotheses
      on <m>\alpha</m> enter, and to which of these approaches the
      course text takes.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-br-4">
    <title>The Stieltjes Integrator</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Bauldry defines the Riemann-Stieltjes integral
        <m>\int_a^b f\,d\alpha</m>. What plays the role of
        <q><m>dx</m></q> in this definition? How does the ordinary
        Riemann integral arise as a special case?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The role of <m>dx</m> is played by <m>d\alpha</m>. For an
        increasing integrator <m>\alpha</m>, in
        the Darboux upper and lower sums this amounts to replacing
        <m>\Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1}</m> with
        <m>\Delta\alpha_i = \alpha(x_i) - \alpha(x_{i-1}) \geq 0</m>. The
        upper Stieltjes sum is
        <m>U(f,P,\alpha) = \sum M_i\,\Delta\alpha_i</m> and the lower
        sum is <m>L(f,P,\alpha) = \sum m_i\,\Delta\alpha_i</m>.
        (For an integrator of bounded variation, the same replacement is
        made in Riemann-Stieltjes sums
        <m>\sum f(t_i)\,\Delta\alpha_i</m>, as described in
        <xref ref="par-mod8-br-24" text="custom">Bauldry &#xa7;2.4: The
        Riemann-Stieltjes Integral</xref>.)
        The Riemann integral arises as the special case
        <m>\alpha(x) = x</m>, which is increasing, gives
        <m>\Delta\alpha_i = \Delta x_i</m>, and recovers the ordinary
        Darboux upper and lower sums.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-br-5">
    <title>Stieltjes Integrability Criterion and Bounded Variation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            State Bauldry's integrability criterion for the
            Riemann-Stieltjes integral. How does it compare to the
            Darboux integrability criterion in the Module 8 study guide?
          </li>
          <li>
            Bauldry requires that the integrator <m>\alpha</m> is of
            <em>bounded variation</em>. What does this mean? Why
            might an integrator of unbounded variation cause problems?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) For an increasing integrator <m>\alpha</m>, the Stieltjes
        integrability criterion has the same
        structure as the Darboux criterion: a bounded <m>f</m> is
        Riemann-Stieltjes integrable with respect to <m>\alpha</m>
        on <m>[a,b]</m> if and only if for every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>
        there exists a partition <m>P</m> with
        <m>U(f,P,\alpha) - L(f,P,\alpha) \lt \varepsilon</m>. The
        difference is that the weights are <m>\Delta\alpha_i</m>
        instead of <m>\Delta x_i</m>. The hypothesis that
        <m>\alpha</m> is increasing cannot be dropped. Take
        <m>\alpha(x) = -x</m> on <m>[0,1]</m> and <m>f</m> the Dirichlet
        function. Then <m>\Delta\alpha_i = -\Delta x_i</m>, so
        <m>U(f,P,\alpha) = \sum 1 \cdot (-\Delta x_i) = -1</m> and
        <m>L(f,P,\alpha) = 0</m> for every partition <m>P</m>, and
        <m>U(f,P,\alpha) - L(f,P,\alpha) = -1 \lt \varepsilon</m>.
        Yet <m>f</m> is not integrable with respect to <m>\alpha</m>:
        its Riemann-Stieltjes sums
        <m>\sum f(t_i)\,\Delta\alpha_i</m> equal <m>-1</m> when every
        <m>t_i</m> is rational and <m>0</m> when every <m>t_i</m> is
        irrational, however fine the partition. For an integrator of
        bounded variation, upper and lower sums are not used directly
        (see <xref ref="par-mod8-br-24" text="custom">Bauldry &#xa7;2.4:
        The Riemann-Stieltjes Integral</xref>).
        (b) A function <m>\alpha</m> is of bounded variation on
        <m>[a,b]</m> if the total variation
        <m>V(\alpha,[a,b]) = \sup_P \sum_{i=1}^n |\alpha(x_i) -
        \alpha(x_{i-1})|</m> is finite. For bounded <m>f</m>, every
        Riemann-Stieltjes sum satisfies
        <md>\Bigl|\sum f(t_i)\,\Delta\alpha_i\Bigr| \leq \Bigl(\sup_{[a,b]}|f|\Bigr) \sum |\Delta\alpha_i|,</md>
        and when <m>\alpha</m> has bounded variation the right side is at
        most <m>\bigl(\sup|f|\bigr)\,V(\alpha,[a,b])</m>. Estimates of this
        kind are what control Stieltjes sums in the existence proofs. If
        <m>\alpha</m> has unbounded variation, the sums
        <m>\sum |\Delta\alpha_i|</m> are unbounded as <m>P</m> varies
        (even if <m>\alpha</m> itself is bounded), and this estimate gives
        no control at all; that is why the existence theorems assume
        bounded variation.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-br-6">
    <title>Continuous Functions are Stieltjes-Integrable</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the theorem in Bauldry &#xa7;2.4 corresponding to the
        statement <q>continuous functions are Riemann integrable</q>
        (Worked Example 2 of this module). What additional hypothesis
        does the Stieltjes setting require on <m>\alpha</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The corresponding theorem in &#xa7;2.4 typically states: if
        <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and <m>\alpha</m> is
        of bounded variation on <m>[a,b]</m>, then <m>f</m> is
        Riemann-Stieltjes integrable with respect to <m>\alpha</m>.
        The additional hypothesis beyond the Riemann setting is that
        <m>\alpha</m> must be of bounded variation; in the Riemann
        integral, <m>\alpha(x) = x</m> automatically has this
        property (it is monotone increasing), but a general integrator
        may not.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-br-7">
    <title>Integration by Parts in the Stieltjes Setting</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Bauldry &#xa7;2.4 contains a version of integration by
            parts for Stieltjes integrals. State it. How does it
            differ from the version you proved in Practice Problem S2?
          </li>
          <li>
            Why does the Stieltjes setting make integration by parts
            more symmetric between <m>f</m> and <m>\alpha</m>?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) The Stieltjes integration by parts formula: if <m>f</m> is
        Riemann-Stieltjes integrable with respect to <m>\alpha</m> on
        <m>[a,b]</m>, then <m>\alpha</m> is Riemann-Stieltjes integrable
        with respect to <m>f</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>, and
        <md>
          \int_a^b f\,d\alpha + \int_a^b \alpha\,df
          = f(b)\alpha(b) - f(a)\alpha(a).
        </md>
        So if either integral exists, so does the other. (For a proof,
        with the integral defined through Riemann-Stieltjes sums, see
        Apostol, <pubtitle>Mathematical Analysis</pubtitle>, 2nd
        edition, Chapter 7; compare the hypotheses with the version in
        the course text.)
        This differs from the ordinary version in that both integrals
        are Stieltjes integrals, and the roles of <m>f</m> and
        <m>\alpha</m> are completely symmetric: <m>f</m> acts as
        integrand in one and as integrator in the other.
        (b) In the ordinary Riemann setting, integration by parts
        involves <m>\int u\,v'\,dx</m> (a Riemann integral of <m>u</m>
        against the derivative of <m>v</m>), which breaks the symmetry
        between <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>. In the Stieltjes setting,
        <m>\int f\,d\alpha</m> and <m>\int \alpha\,df</m> appear
        symmetrically without requiring differentiability; the formula
        requires only that one of the two Stieltjes integrals exists (then, by
        (a), so does the other). One sufficient condition is that
        <m>f</m> is continuous and <m>\alpha</m> is of bounded variation
        on <m>[a,b]</m> (the theorem in <xref ref="ex-mod8-br-6"/>; see
        also Apostol, Chapter 7). Having no common discontinuities is
        not enough: <m>\alpha(x) = x</m> is continuous everywhere, so it
        shares no discontinuity with the Dirichlet function <m>f</m>, but
        <m>\int_0^1 f\,d\alpha</m> is then the Riemann integral of
        <m>f</m>, which does not exist (<xref ref="ex-mod8-sg-52-2"/>).
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       CLOSING
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod8-br-closing">
    <title>Looking Ahead to MAT 5610</title>
    <p>
      MAT 5610 will begin its integration theory with Bauldry &#xa7;2.4.
      The module you have just completed gives you the Darboux framework:
      partitions, upper and lower sums, the integrability criterion, the
      main classes of integrable functions, and the Fundamental Theorem
      of Calculus. The graduate course adds the Stieltjes integrator
      <m>d\alpha</m>, develops the theory of bounded variation, and
      proves deeper results including the Lebesgue criterion for
      Riemann integrability (a bounded function is Riemann integrable
      if and only if it is continuous except on a set of measure zero).
      Come in knowing the integrability criterion cold, and be ready to
      extend it to the Stieltjes setting on the first day.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

</section>
